{"city":{"id":"hong-kong","name":"Hong Kong","country":"China","timezone":"Asia/Hong_Kong","locale":"en-HK","description":"Victoria Harbour from open data: Central, Admiralty and Wan Chai, Tsim Sha Tsui and the Peak, every building at the height the Lands Department surveyed, on the government's LiDAR terrain and under the real sun.","vertical_datum":"mPD","year":2026,"tour":[{"title":"Victoria Harbour from open data","text":"{buildings} buildings from OpenStreetMap and the Lands Department, standing on the government's 2020 LiDAR terrain under the real sun. {share:survey}% of the footprint area has a surveyed top level, and {share:lidar}% more is measured from the LiDAR.","cam":[-1450,-2150,700],"tgt":[-150,250,60],"date":[11,15],"from":"14:00","to":"15:00","hold":9},{"title":"Heights you can check","text":"Blue is a measured height. The Lands Department surveys the top level of every building block; where its blocks miss a footprint, the 2020 LiDAR scan measures the roof. Against the laser, the surveyed roofs agree to {lidar:median_abs_m} m at the median; against the published list of tallest buildings, to {acc:median_abs_error_pct}%.","cam":[500,-1500,950],"tgt":[-300,-300,80],"date":[11,15],"from":"11:00","to":"12:00","hold":10,"provenance":true},{"title":"Bank of China Tower","text":"I. M. Pei's tower is a 52 m square cut along its diagonals into four triangular shafts that stop at four heights, the lowest on the harbour side, the last at the 315 m roof. Its faces are turned {landmark:bank_of_china.bearing_deg}\u00b0 from north, measured on its footprint. The masts reach 367.4 m.","cam":[300,-1000,330],"tgt":[-199,-745,190],"date":[11,15],"from":"10:30","to":"11:30","hold":9},{"title":"Statue Square at midwinter","text":"On 21 December the noon sun stands 44\u00b0 above the southern horizon, behind the bank towers. {sun:Statue Square|12-21|12:00}% of Statue Square is in direct sun at noon. Hong Kong has no rule that protects the sun on its public squares; the model can still measure it.","place":"Statue Square","date":[12,21],"from":"11:00","to":"14:00","hold":10},{"title":"The Star Ferry crossing","text":"About a kilometre of harbour between Central's Pier 7 and Tsim Sha Tsui. The water moves with an easterly blowing down the harbour; the piers and the shoreline are OpenStreetMap's.","cam":[150,350,140],"tgt":[520,840,8],"date":[11,15],"from":"15:30","to":"16:30","hold":9},{"title":"Two IFC and the ICC","text":"The two tallest buildings in the model face each other across the harbour: Two IFC at 412 m and the ICC at 484 m, built to their published heights and crowns. The Lands Department's survey puts their main roofs 11 m and 1 m from those figures.","cam":[900,600,320],"tgt":[-400,800,220],"date":[11,15],"from":"16:00","to":"17:00","hold":9},{"title":"The Peak at dusk","text":"The Peak Tram climbs some 360 m from Garden Road to the Peak Tower, whose crescent roof stands 428 m above the datum. After dark the towers' windows light up across the water.","cam":[-1250,-1400,500],"tgt":[-250,300,90],"date":[11,15],"from":"17:15","to":"18:30","hold":11}],"start":{"date":[11,15],"time":"14:30","cam":[-1450,-2150,700],"tgt":[-150,250,60]},"light_show":{"name":"A Symphony of Lights","start":"20:00","minutes":10,"beams":["bank_of_china","hsbc","ifc2","central_plaza","icc"],"lasers":[["icc","ifc2"],["icc","bank_of_china"],["icc","central_plaza"]],"note":"A Symphony of Lights has run at 8 pm nightly since 2004, with lights on some 40 buildings on both sides of the harbour (Wikipedia; Tourism Commission). In February 2026 the government said it would be retired in the second half of 2026 (Hong Kong Free Press, 27 February 2026); it was still running in September. The beams, lasers and colours here are illustrative, not the show's choreography."},"minor_labels":["Legislative Council","Court of Final Appeal","Ocean Terminal","Central Star Ferry Pier","TST Star Ferry Pier","Xiqu Centre","M+","Cultural Centre","Jardine House","Exchange 